# Airness Payload Layer Guide

This guide applies to every module that inherits `airness-parent-payload`. It adds to the generic guide and to
the node rules; it never relaxes them. A Payload module is a node project whose `package.json` declares
`payload` and `@airness/payload`, built with `pom` packaging.

## Verification

The Payload gates run through `airness:node-gate` like every node gate, bound by `airness-parent-payload`:

| Gate | Phase | What it checks |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `preflight` | `process-sources` | The node project holds a Payload application, and every Payload application declares `payload`, `graphql`, `sharp`, `next`, `react` and `react-dom` at the versions the layer pins; every `@payloadcms/*` package is at the Payload version. |
| `payload-blocklist` | `post-integration-test` | The lockfile resolves no package the Payload layer refuses: the MongoDB adapter, and the drivers of database servers Payload has no adapter for. |
| `payload-source-rules` | `post-integration-test` | Local API calls state their bounds, thread `req`, and decide access; every handler an `endpoints:` entry serves comes from `src/endpoints`; every collection and global decides its access; auth collections lock accounts; uploads are restricted; a configuration that pushes no schema records its migrations; nothing writes at boot. |
| `admin-views` | `post-integration-test` | Every custom admin view has an accessibility specification of the project's own. |
| `payload-environment` | `post-integration-test` | Every variable `src/lib/environment.ts` reads is documented in the member's or the repository's example file, and every `NEXT_PUBLIC_` variable it inlines is declared by an `ARG` in each Dockerfile that builds the member. |
| `payload-generated` | `post-integration-test` | Regenerating the Payload types, the admin import map and, when the member keeps `src/payload-generated-schema.ts`, the database schema with the project's own Payload CLI leaves them unchanged. |
| `payload-defaults` | `post-integration-test` | Every collection and global of the built configuration, framework-built ones included, decides its access; no drafts read serves an unapproved document to a stranger; production authentication limits are valid; every auth collection's session cookie is Secure in production, through `auth.cookies.secure` (Payload's default is `false`); `admin.autoLogin` is off; no upload collection stores its files on the local disk unless `localStorage` declares it. |
| `payload-migrations` | `post-integration-test` | The schema the configuration builds matches the newest `.json` snapshot the committed migrations record, compared as `payload migrate:create` compares it, without a database and without writing a file. |

Run one gate with `pnpm exec airness-payload gate <id> --verbose` to diagnose it. That is a diagnosis, never a verdict.

The node gates apply the Payload layer's additions too. The `eslint` gate runs the node rules plus the Payload
blocks below, and the Extended `e2e` gate runs the layer's `access-boundary` specification, which asks Payload's
`/api/access` what an anonymous caller may do and fails on every grant `publicAccess` does not declare. The
browser sweep scans a custom admin view within Payload's template: the layer supplies the template's content
selectors through `airness.layer.browser`, so Payload's own navigation and header are not judged as the view's.
The Extended `image` gate asks a booted image for `/api/users/init` beside the origin's own path, through the
layer's `imageProbes`: the admin panel asks it whether a first user must be created, and Payload answers it by
reading the users collection, its default auth collection, so an image whose empty database never received its
schema answers 500 and fails. An admin page is no such probe, since Next.js has already sent its 200 when the
query behind it fails.

An image of a Payload application passes that gate when:

- its Dockerfile builds with no build argument: the stage that runs `next build` installs the dependencies from the
  lockfile and gives the variables the configuration validates as it is imported inert placeholders on that `RUN`
  line alone, as `analysisEnv` gives them to the gates, never a real value;
- every directory it copies exists in the build, so a `public` directory is copied only where the project has one;
- the configuration applies the committed migrations as the server starts, with `prodMigrations` set to the
  `migrations` export of `src/migrations`, because the runtime stage holds the built server alone;
- the origin is read at runtime from a variable `serverUrlVariables` names, which the gate sets in the running
  container, rather than set only in the stage that builds.

## Settings

The Payload settings live under the `airnessPayload` key of the member's `package.json`; the node settings stay
under `airness`.

| Setting | Default | Meaning |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `publicAccess` | empty | Entries with `entity`, `operation`, `reason` and optional `fields`, declaring the anonymous permissions the project grants on purpose. An entry missing a field is dropped. |
| `localStorage` | empty | Entries with `collection` and `reason`, naming the upload collections the project keeps on the server's local disk on purpose, such as a single server whose upload directory is a persistent, backed-up volume. An entry missing a field is dropped, and one naming no upload collection on the local disk is a finding. |
| `analysisEnv` | placeholders for `PAYLOAD_SECRET` and `DATABASE_URL` | Variables the gates that import the configuration use when neither the process nor `.env.local`/`.env` supplies them, the node layer's `knip` among them. They let the configuration evaluate; nothing connects to them. |

The layer contributes defaults to the node settings of every member that declares `@airness/payload`: the generated
files (`src/payload-types.ts`, `src/payload-generated-schema.ts`, everything under `src/app/(payload)`, the
admin and API glue Payload scaffolds as generated, and the `src/migrations/*.json` schema snapshots), the framework
glue (`src/payload.config.ts`), the pure-logic floor (`src/access`, `src/lib`), the routes the accessibility
sweep leaves to Payload (`/admin`, `/api`), and the production entry points of the dead-code gate's production run:
the configuration, which the admin panel and the API boot from, and the migrations under `src/migrations`, which
`payload migrate` runs. What only the configuration reaches, such as a collection, a hook or a helper, is therefore
production code. Agents may not edit the generated files; regenerate them with the
Payload CLI. The installed layer contributes exact security replacements for
affected Payload dependency edges through `airness.layer.securityOverrides`; a project may use only those approvals
and the generic Airness approvals.

## Application Code

- Keep environment reads in `src/lib/environment.ts`, validate them there, and document each variable in the
  example file beside the member's `.env`, or in the one at the repository root. The `payload-environment` gate
  knows a variable by the name the module reads it by: a bracketed read, `process.env['NAME']` or `source['NAME']`
  on a parameter that holds the environment, or a name destructured out of either. A schema library handed the
  whole object, as in `schema.parse(process.env)`, names no variable to the gate, so read each variable by its
  name.
- Collection, global, field and block definitions are declarative. Define access rules and pure logic in
  `src/access` or `src/lib`, hooks in `src/hooks`, and import them by reference.
- A collection decides `create`, `read`, `update` and `delete`; a global decides `read` and `update`.
- An auth collection sets `auth.cookies.secure` to true in production, for example from a value
  `src/lib/environment.ts` derives from `NODE_ENV`, because Payload's default sends the session cookie over plain
  HTTP. `payload-defaults` evaluates the configuration with `NODE_ENV` set to `production`.
- `auth.maxLoginAttempts` counts failures only against an account that exists, so an attempt naming an unknown
  email is never limited, while it still pays for the decoy hash that keeps its refusal as slow as any other. The
  limit on repeated authentication failures that the section Authentication of the governing standard asks for
  is therefore the project's own to add for those attempts, for example per client in front of the sign-in route.
- At a Local API call use an explicit options object. A read states its `depth` or `limit`; a call through
  `req.payload` or `request.payload` passes `req`; a call carrying `user`, a call composed with a spread, and
  every call in an endpoint module, a route handler, a server action (a `'use server'` module or function) or a
  page, layout, template, default or not-found module of the App Router outside `src/app/(payload)`, its
  `generateMetadata` and `generateStaticParams` included, or in a function of the project one of them calls,
  states `overrideAccess: false` after any spread. A name bound to `await getPayload(...)` is a Local API
  receiver like `payload`.
- Write every handler a collection, global or root `endpoints:` entry serves in a module under `src/endpoints`,
  and import it there by a relative or `@/` path, so the endpoint rule above judges its calls. A handler written
  in place, or imported from `src/lib`, `src/hooks` or another alias, is refused.
- Store every upload collection's files through a storage adapter, such as `@payloadcms/storage-s3` against an
  S3-compatible service (RustFS for local development and verification), never on the server's local disk: a
  replaced container loses those files and a second instance never sees them. Add each new upload collection to
  the adapter's `collections`.
- A required relationship or upload, on a collection or a global, needs a `beforeDelete` hook on its target
  collection that removes the dependants first, unless drafts are enabled on the holder.
- A database adapter that does not push its schema needs a recorded migration for every schema change: after a
  change, run `payload migrate:create <name>` and commit the migration with the `.json` snapshot it writes. A
  deployed server applies them as it starts through `prodMigrations`, as the `image` gate requires.
- ESLint refuses an inline function in a collection, global, field or block configuration, a read of
  `process.env` outside `src/lib/environment.ts` and `src/proxy.ts`, and `payload` taken out of `req` or
  `request`, destructured or bound to a name as in `const cms = req.payload` (call `req.payload` and pass
  `req`). A hook writes request state only on `req.context`. Outside `src/endpoints` nothing reads `req.data`,
  dotted, bracketed, destructured or through `Reflect.get`: it is the raw request body, the parsed body over REST,
  `{ query, variables }` over GraphQL and nothing over the Local API. A hook reads its operation's own arguments,
  `data` in a collection or field hook and `args.data` in `beforeOperation` or `afterOperation`, and a root hook
  such as `afterError` reads what an operation hook of the same request put on `req.context`. A migration keeps
  the snake-case name `payload migrate:create` gives it.

